WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.972 --> 00:00:04.430
SCOTT JOHNSTON:
I'm Scott Johnston.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:05.970
I'm on the Hangouts team.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.670
Thanks for coming
to this session.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:09.560
We're going to talk
about team communications

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.643
and how we've been thinking
about them in G Suite.

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:16.320
I think that one of the early
questions when we started

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.690
working on Hangouts for
productivity and for teams was,

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.090
what really makes
a successful team?

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.500
And you may have seen us publish
within Google a study called

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.240
Aristotle, where we followed
teams over many, many years

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.760
and looked for what
characteristics really

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.600
made a successful team.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.834
We've done that with our
G Suite customers as well.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:42.250
And that's really
where we started

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.180
in terms of getting
to the heart of what

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.121
tools do we need to provide
teams with to make them better

00:00:49.121 --> 00:00:49.620
teams.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.300
And really to make a team of 300
function like a team of three.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.690
And so one of the core--

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:58.890
there's a number of takeaways--

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:01.740
well one of the key
ones for me was really

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:07.900
that there wasn't a lot of
commonality between work styles

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:15.710
or particular behavior
of the teams in terms

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.044
of being highly
communicative or not so,

00:01:20.044 --> 00:01:21.710
or having a certain
set of personalities

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.590
or having a key mix
of personalities.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.390
But really in the
end the foundation

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:27.890
of the most successful
teams that we

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.530
found when we did
research was that it

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:31.860
was driven by experiences.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.100
So their foundations were what--

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.680
the foundations were the
base of how they interacted.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.372
And those came
through in that they

00:01:45.372 --> 00:01:47.080
were able to have
emotional interactions.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.910
So they were able to connect
at a level that was usually

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.400
outside of work.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.540
They had-- they could have
complex conversations that

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:59.570
were often heated and
intense, and people

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:02.720
felt safe having those
kinds of conversations.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.600
And everyone seemed to
have a mix of work styles.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.539
And so they needed tools that
supported those work styles.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:11.060
They had a great deal of control
over deciding when you were

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.830
going to work and
when you weren't.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.100
Excuse me.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.770
And so that really was at the
foundation of the products

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:22.050
that we built, both from
Jamboard through to Hangouts.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.180
And then the other thing
that we just have seen,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.250
and I don't need
to tell you this,

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.380
is just this
proliferation of tools

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.960
that we can use now
to be productive.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.850
And the more tools the better.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.290
But at the same time people
were jumping between and having

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:42.732
trouble staying in the loop.

00:02:42.732 --> 00:02:44.440
Even something as
simple as a spreadsheet

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.822
to track something
going on requires

00:02:46.822 --> 00:02:48.030
us to go to that spreadsheet.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.750
And there's a
cognitive step there.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.070
And that takes time.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.040
And so we really
needed to figure out

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.080
a way to take communication
and span it across an ecosystem

00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:01.515
maps, not just create a new
home that people would go to,

00:03:01.515 --> 00:03:03.140
and think that would
solve the problem.

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.440
And so what we
announced this morning,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.410
for those of you who
weren't at the keynote,

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.480
is a rebuilt Hangouts.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:19.450
And there are really two
components of team productivity

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:20.680
that we focused on.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.840
One was the virtual
room within chat.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.290
And then the other was meetings,
face-to-face meetings being

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.877
key, getting you into meetings.

00:03:28.877 --> 00:03:31.210
And there was a session earlier
today, maybe some of you

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.517
made on Hangouts Meet.

00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:35.350
But what this session
is going to talk about

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.450
in a bit more
detail is really how

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.872
we rebuild Hangouts chat, the
focus on team productivity.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.620
There were really
three key principles

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.300
that we were working with.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.610
One was this ability to focus.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.000
And there's a couple different
aspects around focus and teams.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.850
One is your ability to turn
off whatever communication tool

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:59.700
that you're using so
it doesn't bother you,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.730
and you can focus
and get into flow.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.950
But also, even within
the communication tool,

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:06.750
we knew we had to
figure out a way

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.690
that, especially in real
time communications,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.460
a way to allow people to have
conversations that didn't

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.710
interrupt other conversations.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.320
And we've all seen this
in consumer chat tools,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.501
where conversation will get
woven and fragmented, really.

00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:21.959
You'll be deep in
one conversation,

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.470
and another person will
bring up another topic.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.580
And that fragments
the conversation

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.710
and often is very
frustrating to users.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:33.120
So we had to figure out a
way to get a form of focus

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.030
on the ability to drill
into a particular topic

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.350
without interrupting others.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:39.390
Expression was key.

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.080
And this is linked to what
I was talking about earlier,

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.690
around teams really
needing to be

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.610
able to connect at
an experience level

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.060
and at an emotional level.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:51.970
And then the other core part was
connecting the different tools,

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:54.370
bringing them into the same UI.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.860
And then automation was core.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:57.941
And that really--

00:04:57.941 --> 00:05:00.190
I know we talk at Google a
lot about machine learning,

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:03.070
but automation actually starts
earlier than machine learning.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.789
Which is-- even our own
products, for example,

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:07.330
take way too many
steps for you guys,

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.430
for you folks to do things.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.160
If you're in a six person
meeting, creating a Docs file

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:13.720
and sharing it with
those six people

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.700
is way too many steps right now.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.160
And so there's a
coarse automation focus

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.040
we needed to have to
start to automatically do

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.890
things that require too
many steps right now.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:26.230
And that's just a simple
computer science problem, not

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:27.760
a machine learning problem.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.800
But we knew we needed
to start there.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.425
And that's where, when you look
at some of what we talked about

00:05:32.425 --> 00:05:36.850
this morning around the Meet
agent that automatically

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.011
schedules things for you,
that's what that's about,

00:05:39.011 --> 00:05:40.510
is how do we eliminate
as many steps

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:41.885
from your day as
possible, so you

00:05:41.885 --> 00:05:43.220
can focus on the work at hand?

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.100
Like other products
in G Suite we

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.210
knew we had a strong
focus on the enterprise.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:57.120
That's everything from
security to scalability.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.540
And scalability doesn't
just mean classic fan out

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.550
and ability to handle
a lot of users,

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.200
but also the ability
to really work

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.570
within an organization
in a scalable way.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:11.460
And so what you'll learn
about Hangouts Chat is

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:16.080
that the structures of it can
be wired up to Google Groups

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.780
or connected to central
identity systems

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:23.010
such that you can
have rooms configured

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.200
around specific teams
and departments,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.620
such that you're not
dealing with this giant pile

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.210
of discussion rooms.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.040
But instead it's divided
into topical areas

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:36.312
that are relevant to your team.

00:06:36.312 --> 00:06:37.770
And what that means
is that you can

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:41.490
scale across an enterprise
and not just work

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.200
in a small business setting.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.710
Accessibility is core to
everything we do in G Suite.

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.430
And so that's the foundation
of everything in design

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.540
from the very beginning.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.640
Reliability, ease of
use, these are things

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.640
that are just part of our DNA.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.660
And so we're going to talk now--
we're going to bring up Mike,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.160
who works within
the Hangouts team--

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:09.970
and we're going to walk
through a more detailed demo

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:15.580
of Hangouts chat, just
to dive into details

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.570
beyond this morning.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:20.020
MIKE SORVILLO: MIKE: Cool.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:22.060
Thank you guys.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.280
We all know there's
this perfect process

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.880
that you'll put in place for
a new idea, new products,

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.900
a new project, but never goes
the way you want it to go.

00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:35.290
It's always a bit more organic,
a bit more unstructured.

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.920
And we're going to show
you how a team at Tegu Toys

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.430
uses the new Hangouts
Chat to plan a new product

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:44.530
and get it out to the market.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.590
So can we switch to
the demo, please?

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:51.262
Perfect.

00:07:51.262 --> 00:07:52.720
So the first thing
you'll notice is

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.380
we have a brand new web UI we
just built from the ground up,

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:59.710
completely dedicated to
just a chat interface.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.290
So just like Hangouts today,
we have direct messages

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:04.450
where you can talk one
on one with somebody.

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.820
Right now I'm
talking with Mandy.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:10.480
She put a-- we're just having
a conversation about some

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:11.590
of the existing toys.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.380
And I mentioned that I
have a new idea for a toy

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.390
that I want to work on.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.030
So one of the main things we've
added to the new Hangouts Chat

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.700
are rooms, the ability to
centralize conversations

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:24.140
around projects or teams.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.950
So instead of actually--
thanks, Mandy.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:30.571
Instead of actually having
a conversation right

00:08:30.571 --> 00:08:33.070
here with Mandy, I want to bring
Varun into the conversation

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:33.570
as well.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.770
And I want to create a
room for this new project.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.270
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:39.520
to go down here,
click the Add button,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.220
and create a new
room for the project.

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.670
I'm going to add Mandy and
Varun to the room itself,

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.100
and put a Google doc in the
room that talks a little bit

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.391
more details about the project.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:15.650
Now all I have to do to
bring Mandy into the room

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:18.830
is just at mention
her, and Varun as well.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:23.720
And right away they're brought
into the room and ready to go.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:25.970
The new Hangouts Chat also
lets you add Google Groups

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:26.664
as members.

00:09:26.664 --> 00:09:28.580
So if you use Google
Groups to manage identity

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.270
across your whole
company, you can

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.510
reuse those same groups
to deploy Hangouts Chat

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:35.417
to your entire organization.

00:09:35.417 --> 00:09:37.250
The other thing you'll
notice is that I just

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.110
pasted a Google Docs link.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:41.570
We have deep integration with
Google Drive and Google Docs.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.280
So any link you post
will automatically

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.530
get a beautiful thumbnail
rendered right in line,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.470
so everybody can quickly
get back to files,

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.510
in addition to the
fact that we changed

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.260
the file permissions to
automatically match the room.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.210
So everyone in the
room can have access.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:09:59.000
And we remove the mundane task
of requesting access and giving

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:03.230
access, so people could just
start working immediately.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.690
So this right now,
this is what we

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.610
like to call the seed
of the team, right?

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:08.990
We have an idea.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:10.617
We have some content.

00:10:10.617 --> 00:10:11.450
We have some people.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:13.580
We have some conversation.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.800
And the thing you'll
notice is, we didn't

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.060
need to bother IT about this.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:18.476
We didn't need to
say hey, can you

00:10:18.476 --> 00:10:20.190
please create an
email alias for us?

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.310
Or hey, can you
provision something new

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.010
on our on-prem filer for us.

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.740
We just got together,
started working immediately.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.290
And you can see right now,
everyone's busy at work.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.630
Mandy's putting a bunch
of stuff into the chat.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:35.039
She put a projection
spreadsheet into the chat.

00:10:35.039 --> 00:10:36.830
Varun's putting a Gantt
chart into the chat

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:41.000
for how we can actually get
this project moving forward.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.330
And we recognize that when
you're working on projects

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.880
you're going to be talking
about different things.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.700
So one thing you'll notice
is that rooms are threaded.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:51.290
We let you separate
the conversation

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:53.780
and keep context around
different things.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:56.720
So the conversation about
the projections spreadsheet

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:58.970
can be separated from the
conversation about the Gantt

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:00.680
chart.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.346
And threads are not just
great for separating out

00:11:03.346 --> 00:11:04.970
different topics
within it, but it also

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.730
helps separate the unimportant
chitchat that happens,

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.470
like where do you
want to go for lunch,

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:12.140
or what movie did
you see this weekend,

00:11:12.140 --> 00:11:14.870
from the important work.

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:17.572
Now these threads are not
meant to feel heavyweight.

00:11:17.572 --> 00:11:20.030
They're not meant to feel like
something you need to manage

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:21.410
or you need to organize.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:24.170
We don't want to put
work on the user.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:27.770
So the way they work is whenever
a new thread gets updated, it

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:29.250
bumps down to the bottom.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.410
And you'll get notified.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.016
And you'll be able to see all
the things you haven't read.

00:11:33.016 --> 00:11:34.640
So over time what
ends up happening is,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.150
all the important discussions
bubble up to the bottom.

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:39.564
And all of the
unimportant stuff,

00:11:39.564 --> 00:11:40.980
or the stuff that's
been resolved,

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:44.900
just kind of floats into the
ether of the chat history.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.060
So you'll see right
here that there's

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.690
a bunch of conversation
going on with Mady and Varun

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:51.609
on the Gantt chart.

00:11:51.609 --> 00:11:53.150
And I'm sure everyone
in the audience

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.120
has experienced this before,
where you text a friend,

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:59.300
they text you back, you text
them again, they text you back.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.060
And eventually you're
like OK, please just stop.

00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:03.500
Let's just talk
on the telephone.

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.510
This is taking forever.

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:09.630
So we recognize this happens
at work all the time as well.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:12.650
So what we've done is we've
deeply integrated Hangouts

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:14.570
Meet into Hangouts chat.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:17.870
So when I want to just
stop the chat conversation,

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:20.040
I can click this
button down here

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.640
and insert a video meeting,
write a little note.

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:29.630
Post it into the chat room.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:31.625
And now a video
meeting was created.

00:12:31.625 --> 00:12:33.500
With one click I could
jump into the meeting.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:37.280
And I'm put into a green room
where I can see how I look,

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.170
see who else is in the meeting.

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.790
And then I can jump
into the meeting.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.960
And you'll notice Mandy's
in the meeting right now.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.989
And we could get started.

00:12:45.989 --> 00:12:47.030
So this is Hangouts Meet.

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:49.030
Like Scott mentioned
earlier, hopefully you guys

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:50.270
caught the session earlier.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.520
We talked about it a little
bit at the keynote itself.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:57.932
Oh, everybody left.

00:12:57.932 --> 00:12:59.640
We talked about it at
the keynote itself,

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.710
so I'm not going to spend a lot
of time talking about it right

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:02.520
now.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.680
But at a high level,
specifically designed

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.480
with enterprise use in mind.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.700
So a completely new code base.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:11.010
Less code means
faster CPU speed,

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.470
better performance, and
specific enterprise features,

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:17.760
like one click to present, the
ability for non-Google Apps

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.124
customers to dial
into a meeting,

00:13:20.124 --> 00:13:21.540
and the ability
for people to dial

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.840
in on the road with a
dial-in telephone number.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:28.129
So we were able to take this low
bandwidth conversation in chat

00:13:28.129 --> 00:13:30.420
and move it to a high bandwidth
conversation with video

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:33.240
and resolve it very fast.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:35.970
So some time has
passed, and I want

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.250
to add Nicole to this project.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:42.590
So I'm going to do that on the
new Hangouts Chat mobile apps.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:46.120
Can we switch to the
[INAUDIBLE] vision please?

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:47.960
Great.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:50.200
So-- wonderful.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.870
So I have to do, like
before, when I want

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:54.280
to add someone to the meeting--

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:56.560
to the room, excuse
me, is I just

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.270
need to at mention
them to bring them in.

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:00.710
So we'll type Nicole's name.

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:03.400
I'll add her.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.150
Acknowledge she's going
to be in the room.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.100
And now she's brought
into the room.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.270
And this is where
rooms really shine.

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.630
So when you onboard
new employees,

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.550
there's always a ramp
up period, right?

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:16.040
You need to tell the new
employees what they should

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:17.870
be reading, what
everybody's working on,

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:20.570
the way your team works.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:22.160
You know they spend
some time rifling

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:25.160
through some outdated filers,
looking at stale documents that

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:28.124
really are kind of irrelevant
and don't even matter anymore.

00:14:28.124 --> 00:14:29.540
But the great thing
about this now

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.220
is that Nicole's in this room.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:33.350
She can see the most
recent conversations.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:36.290
She could jump right into the
most recent conversations.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.379
And she could see all
the files as well.

00:14:38.379 --> 00:14:39.920
And the most recent
ones are probably

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.979
the most relevant ones as well.

00:14:41.979 --> 00:14:44.270
And like I mentioned before,
because we have deep Drive

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.610
integration, Nicole already
has access to these files.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.920
So we don't need to
retroactively give her access

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:51.320
to the files, which is great.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.270
So over time this
room becomes very

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:55.120
much like a
centralized team hub,

00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.980
a team repository, where all
the important conversations

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:02.130
you have live, all the files
you've shared live as well.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:06.200
So we've indexed all that and
made it completely searchable.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:09.260
So when I want to
search, just click

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.612
on the little Search button.

00:15:10.612 --> 00:15:13.070
And we know through all of our
Drive research and our Gmail

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.050
research, you can never
remember the file name

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:16.710
of a file someone
shared with you,

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.947
or ever remember the
subject line of an email.

00:15:18.947 --> 00:15:21.530
But you always have those other
little nuggets of information,

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:27.380
like who sent the file, or
what kind of thing they sent.

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.866
So while you can
search for keyword,

00:15:29.866 --> 00:15:31.490
what we've seen in
our trusted testers,

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:35.090
many people actually just search
by person or search by type.

00:15:35.090 --> 00:15:38.930
So when I want to, I can
get back to that document

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:43.750
that I just posted earlier
and the room itself.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:44.250
Cool.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:45.708
Can we switch back
to demo, please?

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:55.230
So we know that with chat apps,
notifications can be a problem.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.420
So we want to make sure
you don't feel like you're

00:15:57.420 --> 00:16:00.290
working all the time.

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:04.050
We have very granular chat
controls, to let you dial up

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:06.484
or dial down the notifications
to as much as you want.

00:16:06.484 --> 00:16:08.400
So if you want to be in
the loop all the time,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:10.390
you can say, send me everything.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:12.867
But if you don't, you
can just say OK, only

00:16:12.867 --> 00:16:14.700
notify me when I was
specifically mentioned.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:16.324
Someone was trying
to get my attention,

00:16:16.324 --> 00:16:18.420
so you know it's a good
time to write back.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:21.330
And not only that,
we have notification

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.532
controls at the
thread level as well.

00:16:23.532 --> 00:16:25.740
So when there becomes a
really unwieldy thread that's

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.630
a little noisy, or
maybe a thread that

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:31.359
is a little bit not
even relevant to you,

00:16:31.359 --> 00:16:33.400
like perhaps I don't care
about this Gantt chart,

00:16:33.400 --> 00:16:36.750
all I have to do is just
mute this thread right here.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.030
And now I will no longer
get any notifications

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:42.060
in that thread at all.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:45.290
So let's fast forward one year.

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:48.870
These colored blocks,
they're in the market.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:54.560
And we are working with a bunch
of other tools to help us.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:58.550
And with these tools,
we've integrated them

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:00.440
using the Hangouts platform.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.653
So they're actually
in the chat UI itself.

00:17:02.653 --> 00:17:04.069
So let me switch
over to the sales

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:07.400
leads room, just
to give an example.

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.609
So our team uses Zendesk
for high touch distribution

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:11.714
partners.

00:17:11.714 --> 00:17:13.589
So these are folks that
are really important.

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:16.130
And we want to know whenever
there's an issue with them,

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:19.280
so we can forward their
ticket and respond promptly.

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:21.770
So Zendesk has
built a Zendesk bot

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:25.940
that notifies people whenever
a high priority item comes in.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.220
So you can see right here
that something was posted.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.100
A high priority ticket
was created from ToyMart.

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:32.900
And the issue is that
a bunch of people

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:36.980
are returning this specific toy
set because parts are missing.

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:38.472
So we need to fix that.

00:17:38.472 --> 00:17:40.430
And we need to rectify
the situation with them,

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:41.686
so they can be happy.

00:17:41.686 --> 00:17:43.810
So right away Patrick just
said, can you take this,

00:17:43.810 --> 00:17:45.200
and forwarded that
over to Scott,

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.080
so he can take it
and jump on it.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:52.270
Our team also uses
ProsperWorks CRM

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:54.820
to keep track of
current customers

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.990
and prospective customers.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.890
So I'm going to switch
over to ProsperWorks now.

00:17:59.890 --> 00:18:01.200
This is our sales pipeline.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:04.560
So you can see all of the
opportunities that we have,

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:08.020
and broken up into all the
different stages right now.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:11.020
We just had a pitch with
Dinosaur Hill Toys in New York

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:13.360
City, and the pitch
went really well.

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.280
So they said they're
going to carry the color

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:16.474
blocks in their store.

00:18:16.474 --> 00:18:17.890
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:20.980
going to move this Dinosaur
Hill Toys from presentation

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.450
into contract sent,
because that's

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:23.616
the stage it's in right now.

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:27.420
So we've moved it.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.170
And now when you go
over to the Chat UI,

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:34.250
you'll notice in real time
the notification was posted,

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:36.890
saying Dinosaur Hill Toys
was changed from presentation

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:39.030
to contract sent.

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:41.330
And now anyone could
start commenting on this.

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.130
Like Mandy just said--

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:45.410
I think that's fireworks, right?

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:49.100
So Mady just posted
some fireworks.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.710
Varun called me a superstar,
which is very sweet.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:55.400
So I could do that in real time.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.580
And not only do we want to make
it so third parties can push

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:00.410
things into the chat
UI, we want to make it

00:19:00.410 --> 00:19:02.750
so users can actually pull
data from third parties

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.340
into the chat UI as well.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.820
So when I want to find out
what my sales numbers were

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:10.390
last month, all I have to do is
talk to the ProsperWorks bot.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.370
And it'll write
back, letting me know

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:30.230
that I closed a total of $4,300
worth of sales last month.

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.060
So can we switch back to
the presentation, please?

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.830
So we're teaming with
a number of companies

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:44.250
to build bots on the
Hangouts Chat platform,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.660
from a number of
business functions.

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:49.710
So regardless of if you work
in sales or marketing, customer

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.579
service, HR, the tools
you use every single day

00:19:52.579 --> 00:19:54.620
will be even more useful
to you in Hangouts Chat.

00:19:57.450 --> 00:20:00.300
Can we switch back to
the presentation, please?

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.420
Demo, sorry.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:11.200
So not only can you work
with these other third party

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:15.130
services, we allow you to easily
build bots using Apps Script.

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:18.440
Which means that you can
write very custom workflow

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.260
specific to your
company, and deploy them

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:21.430
to your entire domain.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.680
So you can do things
like, perhaps you

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.570
have a ticketing system
that's just custom

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:26.789
internal to your company.

00:20:26.789 --> 00:20:29.080
You could write a bot that
lets the user submit tickets

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:30.184
straight from the chat UI.

00:20:30.184 --> 00:20:32.350
You could deploy it to all
the users in your domain.

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:34.420
So on day one they can
use it without having

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:38.450
to go to another external tool.

00:20:38.450 --> 00:20:41.470
So we also use the
chat platform to--

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.669
the Hangouts platform, excuse
me-- to build our own bots.

00:20:44.669 --> 00:20:46.960
So like Scott mentioned
earlier, we demoed the Meet bot

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:48.019
at the keynote.

00:20:48.019 --> 00:20:50.560
And the Meet bot uses natural
language processing and machine

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:56.480
intelligence to automatically
schedule a meeting for us.

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:00.140
So I'm going to switch over to
the Launch Coordination room.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.425
And I think Mandy is going
to show us how it'll work.

00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:11.030
So she asked the Meet
Bot to find a time today.

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:13.089
And it wrote back with a card.

00:21:13.089 --> 00:21:15.380
Now there's actually a lot
of really intelligent things

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:16.100
happening here.

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:18.990
The Meet bot understands
things like today, tomorrow,

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.590
next week, next month,
two months from now.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:24.080
It understands when
people are on vacation,

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.650
when people are in the office.

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:27.564
It understands what time
zones people are in.

00:21:27.564 --> 00:21:29.480
So if I have a meeting
with someone in Zurich,

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.402
we're not scheduling that
meeting at 11:30 PM for them.

00:21:32.402 --> 00:21:34.610
You can do other things,
like reschedule the meeting.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.680
So Mady moved the
meeting to tomorrow.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:41.805
It looked at the calendars.

00:21:41.805 --> 00:21:44.732
It rescheduled the
meeting to tomorrow.

00:21:44.732 --> 00:21:46.440
It supports other
commands in the future,

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:49.110
like canceling the meeting,
adding additional people

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.330
to the meeting,
meeting right now,

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:53.700
and also just booking a
room for you, if you want

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.200
to just huddle really quick.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:59.354
We're also going to be building
other bots in the future that

00:21:59.354 --> 00:22:01.770
are very intelligent on top
of the Hangouts chat platform,

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.810
that we're going to be excited
to share with you in the coming

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:04.680
months.

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.435
So I'm going to turn
it back over to Scott

00:22:06.435 --> 00:22:10.672
to jump back into the slides.

00:22:10.672 --> 00:22:11.660
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:15.782 --> 00:22:16.990
SCOTT JOHNSTON: Thanks, Mike.

00:22:16.990 --> 00:22:18.810
That was great.

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:21.510
So that's at a high
level just a walk through

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:25.570
of the new Hangouts chat.

00:22:25.570 --> 00:22:28.440
And three main areas
that we focused on,

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.410
direct messages,
which we support now

00:22:30.410 --> 00:22:33.350
in classic Hangouts,
along with Presence,

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:36.360
we added virtual team
rooms for huddling around

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:42.420
projects, teams, topics, and
a new Hangouts platform that

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.090
supports everything from
scripting with Google Apps

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.260
Script through to
intelligent bots.

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.730
Some of the key features
that we're launching

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.670
are ranged from search,
everything from free text

00:22:56.670 --> 00:23:00.480
search to, as Mike
touched on, filtering

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:04.440
by people and types of
files, custom workflows

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:08.490
with Apps Script, Docs and
Drive integration, the Hangouts

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:11.100
platform itself, the
Meet bot that comes

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.460
with intelligent scheduling--

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:17.610
with Hangouts chat and does
intelligent scheduling,

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.865
and enterprise readiness
across the Hangouts platform.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:27.330
So high level, looking at
helping you connect easily,

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:31.350
get stuff done, and be
ready for your enterprise.

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:33.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

